= Connectivity (graph theory) — Simple =

'''Connectivity''' in graph theory asks whether vertices in a graph can reach one another through paths.

Imagine a road map.

If every town can reach every other town using the roads, the graph is connected.

If one group of towns has no road path to another group, the graph is disconnected.

== Connected components ==

A '''connected component''' is one maximal connected part of a graph.

If a network has three separate islands of connected vertices, it has three connected components.

An isolated vertex counts as its own component.

== Directed graphs are different ==

In a directed graph, edges have arrows.

A path from A to B does not necessarily mean there is a path back from B to A.

A directed graph is '''strongly connected''' when every vertex can reach every other vertex in both directions.

It is '''weakly connected''' when it becomes connected after ignoring the arrow directions.

== Articulation points ==

An '''articulation vertex''' is a vertex whose removal disconnects the graph.

It is a single point of failure.

For example, one airport might be the only connection between two regional flight networks.

Removing it separates the network.

== Bridges ==

A '''bridge''' is an edge whose removal disconnects the graph.

A bridge can be thought of as the only road between two regions.

Trees are connected graphs with no cycles.

Every edge of a tree is therefore a bridge.

== Redundant paths ==

Cycles create alternative routes.

If one edge on a cycle fails, another path may still connect the same regions.

This makes the graph more robust.

But several routes can still share the same vulnerable vertex.

== Vertex connectivity ==

'''Vertex connectivity''' asks:

<pre>
What is the smallest number of vertices that must be removed to disconnect the graph?
</pre>

A graph that requires several vertex removals before disconnection is more robust to node failure than one with an articulation point.

== Edge connectivity ==

'''Edge connectivity''' asks the same question for edges.

For a connected simple graph,

<math>\kappa(G)\le\lambda(G)\le\delta(G),</math>

where <math>\kappa</math> is vertex connectivity, <math>\lambda</math> edge connectivity, and <math>\delta</math> minimum degree.<ref name="diestel">[https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-662-53622-3 Diestel, ''Graph Theory''.]</ref>

== Menger's theorem ==

Menger's theorem connects two ideas:

* minimum cuts;
* maximum numbers of disjoint paths.

Roughly, if many independent paths connect two vertices, many vertices or edges must be removed to separate them.

This gives a mathematical link between redundancy and robustness.

== Finding connected components ==

Breadth-first search and depth-first search can find the connected component containing a starting vertex.

With adjacency lists, they run in:

<math>O(|V|+|E|).</math>

Repeating from unvisited vertices finds all components.

== Strongly connected components ==

Directed graphs need different algorithms for strongly connected components.

Tarjan's and Kosaraju's algorithms can find them in linear time.

If every strongly connected component is collapsed into one node, the result is a directed acyclic graph.

== Connected does not mean fast ==

A network can be connected but still have:

* very long paths;
* bottlenecks;
* slow links;
* low bandwidth.

Connectivity only says that a path exists.

Other graph measures are needed for distance and capacity.

== Connected does not mean reliable ==

A network can be connected because of one fragile bridge.

Random failures and targeted failures can affect the network differently.

Robustness claims should say which kind of failure is being studied.

== Time can matter ==

A communication link may exist only at certain times.

If we combine all links into one static graph, the network can look connected even though no usable sequence of links exists at the right times.

Temporal networks therefore need time-respecting paths.

